```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r6
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies on R6, not R7. R7's OSPF process is running correctly — it has a
valid router ID (67.1.1.2), all six interfaces in area 0.0.0.1 (the 67.0.0.0/8
link to R6, the 70.0.0.0/8 LAN, and four 17.x.x.x loopbacks), and is actively
sending Hellos. The problem is that R6's ospfd daemon has been killed. R6's
startup config (/etc/frr/frr.conf) is intact and correct — it advertises
16/46/56/60/67.0.0.0/8 into OSPF area 1 — and /etc/frr/daemons has ospfd=yes,
but the ospfd process is simply absent from R6's process table. Only zebra,
mgmtd, and staticd are running on R6.

With no ospfd, R6 cannot form an adjacency with R7 on segment Y (67.0.0.0/8).
R7 therefore has zero OSPF neighbors and cannot inject any LSAs into the domain.
Upstream routers (R4, R5) also lost their adjacency with R6, so R6's own prefixes
vanished as well. The net effect is that all R7-originated routes — the four
17.x.x.x loopbacks, the 67.0.0.0/8 R6-R7 link, and the 70.0.0.0/8 LAN —
disappear from R1's RIB and every other router in the topology.

Root cause: The ospfd process on R6 was killed while the rest of FRR remained
running, silently removing R6 from the OSPF domain and isolating R7.

Corrective fix: Restart ospfd on R6. Running `systemctl restart frr` (or
`/usr/lib/frr/frrinit.sh restart` inside the container) will re-launch ospfd,
reload the existing frr.conf, re-establish adjacencies with R4, R5, and R7, and
restore all missing routes within one OSPF Hello/Dead interval cycle.
=== END ===
```